.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt EVP_PKEY-RSA 7ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm EVP_PKEY-RSA ,
.Nm EVP_KEYMGMT-RSA ,
.Nm RSA
.Nd EVP_PKEY RSA keytype and algorithm support
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Sy RSA
keytype is implemented in OpenSSL's default and FIPS providers.
That implementation supports the basic RSA keys, containing the modulus
.Em n ,
the public exponent
.Em e ,
the private exponent
.Em d ,
and a collection of prime factors, exponents and coefficient for CRT
calculations, of which the first few are known as
.Em p
and
.Em q ,
.Em dP
and
.Em dQ ,
and
.Em qInv .
.Ss Common RSA parameters
In addition to the common parameters that all keytypes should support
(see
.Xr provider-keymgmt 7 Ns ),
the
.Sy RSA
keytype implementation supports the following.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \(dqn" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_N Ns ) <unsigned integer>
The RSA modulus "n" value.
.It \(dqe" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_E Ns ) <unsigned integer>
The RSA public exponent "e" value.
This value must always be set when creating a raw key using
.Xr EVP_PKEY_fromdata 3 .
Note that when a decryption operation is performed, that this value is
used for blinding purposes to prevent timing attacks.
.It \(dqd" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_D Ns ) <unsigned integer>
The RSA private exponent "d" value.
.It \(dqrsa-factor1" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_FACTOR1 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-factor2" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_FACTOR2 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-factor3" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_FACTOR3 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-factor4" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_FACTOR4 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-factor5" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_FACTOR5 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-factor6" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_FACTOR6 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-factor7" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_FACTOR7 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-factor8" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_FACTOR8 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-factor9" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_FACTOR9 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-factor10" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_FACTOR10 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
RSA prime factors.
The factors are known as "p", "q" and "r_i" in RFC8017. Up to eight
additional "r_i" prime factors are supported.
.It \(dqrsa-exponent1" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_EXPONENT1 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-exponent2" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_EXPONENT2 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-exponent3" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_EXPONENT3 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-exponent4" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_EXPONENT4 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-exponent5" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_EXPONENT5 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-exponent6" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_EXPONENT6 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-exponent7" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_EXPONENT7 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-exponent8" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_EXPONENT8 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-exponent9" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_EXPONENT9 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-exponent10" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_EXPONENT10 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
RSA CRT (Chinese Remainder Theorem) exponents.
The exponents are known as "dP", "dQ" and "d_i" in RFC8017. Up to eight
additional "d_i" exponents are supported.
.It \(dqrsa-coefficient1" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_COEFFICIENT1 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-coefficient2" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_COEFFICIENT2 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-coefficient3" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_COEFFICIENT3 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-coefficient4" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_COEFFICIENT4 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-coefficient5" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_COEFFICIENT5 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-coefficient6" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_COEFFICIENT6 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-coefficient7" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_COEFFICIENT7 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-coefficient8" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_COEFFICIENT8 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqrsa-coefficient9" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_COEFFICIENT9 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
RSA CRT (Chinese Remainder Theorem) coefficients.
The coefficients are known as "qInv" and "t_i".
Up to eight additional "t_i" exponents are supported.
.El
.Ss RSA key generation parameters
When generating RSA keys, the following key generation parameters may be
used.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \(dqbits" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_BITS Ns ) <unsigned integer>
The value should be the cryptographic length for the
.Sy RSA
cryptosystem, in bits.
.It \(dqprimes" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_PRIMES Ns ) <unsigned integer>
The value should be the number of primes for the generated
.Sy RSA
key.
The default is 2. It isn't permitted to specify a larger number of
primes than 10. Additionally, the number of primes is limited by the
length of the key being generated so the maximum number could be less.
Some providers may only support a value of 2.
.It \(dqe" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_E Ns ) <unsigned integer>
The RSA "e" value.
The value may be any odd number greater than or equal to 65537. The
default value is 65537. For legacy reasons a value of 3 is currently
accepted but is deprecated.
.It \(dqrsa-derive-from-pq" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_DERIVE_FROM_PQ Ns ) <unsigned integer>
Indicate that missing parameters not passed in the parameter list should
be derived if not provided.
Setting a nonzero value will cause all needed exponents and coefficients
to be derived if not available.
Setting this option requires at least OSSL_PARAM_RSA_FACTOR1,
OSSL_PARAM_RSA_FACTOR2, and OSSL_PARAM_RSA_N to be provided.
This option is ignored if OSSL_KEYMGMT_SELECT_PRIVATE_KEY is not set in
the selection parameter.
.El
.Ss RSA key generation parameters for FIPS module testing
When generating RSA keys, the following additional key generation
parameters may be used for algorithm testing purposes only.
Do not use these to generate RSA keys for a production environment.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \(dqxp" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_TEST_XP Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqxq" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_TEST_XQ Ns ) <unsigned integer>
These 2 fields are normally randomly generated and are used to generate
"p" and "q".
.It \(dqxp1" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_TEST_XP1 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqxp2" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_TEST_XP2 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqxq1" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_TEST_XQ1 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqxq2" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_TEST_XQ2 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
These 4 fields are normally randomly generated.
The prime factors "p1", "p2", "q1" and "q2" are determined from these
values.
.El
.Ss RSA key parameters for FIPS module testing
The following intermediate values can be retrieved only if the values
specified in
.Sx "RSA key generation parameters for FIPS module testing"
are set.
These should not be accessed in a production environment.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \(dqp1" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_TEST_P1 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqp2" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_TEST_P2 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqq1" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_TEST_Q1 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
.It \(dqq2" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_TEST_Q2 Ns ) <unsigned integer>
The auxiliary probable primes.
.El
.Ss RSA key validation
For RSA keys,
.Xr EVP_PKEY_param_check 3
and
.Xr EVP_PKEY_param_check_quick 3
both return 1 unconditionally.
.Pp
For RSA keys,
.Xr EVP_PKEY_public_check 3
conforms to the SP800-56Br1
.Em public key check
when the OpenSSL FIPS provider is used.
The OpenSSL default provider performs similar tests but relaxes the
keysize restrictions for backwards compatibility.
.Pp
For RSA keys,
.Xr EVP_PKEY_public_check_quick 3
is the same as
.Xr EVP_PKEY_public_check 3 .
.Pp
For RSA keys,
.Xr EVP_PKEY_private_check 3
conforms to the SP800-56Br1
.Em private key test .
.Pp
For RSA keys,
.Xr EVP_PKEY_pairwise_check 3
conforms to the SP800-56Br1
.Em KeyPair Validation check
for the OpenSSL FIPS provider.
The OpenSSL default provider allows testing of the validity of
multi-primes.
.Sh CONFORMING TO
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It FIPS186-4
Section B.3.6 Generation of Probable Primes with Conditions Based on
Auxiliary Probable Primes
.It RFC 8017, excluding RSA-PSS and RSA-OAEP
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
An
.Sy EVP_PKEY
context can be obtained by calling:
.Bd -literal
    EVP_PKEY_CTX *pctx =
        EVP_PKEY_CTX_new_from_name(NULL, "RSA", NULL);
.Ed
.Pp
An
.Sy RSA
key can be generated simply like this:
.Bd -literal
    pkey = EVP_RSA_gen(4096);
.Ed
.Pp
or like this:
.Bd -literal
    EVP_PKEY *pkey = NULL;
    EVP_PKEY_CTX *pctx =
        EVP_PKEY_CTX_new_from_name(NULL, "RSA", NULL);

    EVP_PKEY_keygen_init(pctx);
    EVP_PKEY_generate(pctx, &pkey);
    EVP_PKEY_CTX_free(pctx);
.Ed
.Pp
An
.Sy RSA
key can be generated with key generation parameters:
.Bd -literal
    unsigned int primes = 3;
    unsigned int bits = 4096;
    OSSL_PARAM params[3];
    EVP_PKEY *pkey = NULL;
    EVP_PKEY_CTX *pctx = EVP_PKEY_CTX_new_from_name(NULL, "RSA", NULL);

    EVP_PKEY_keygen_init(pctx);

    params[0] = OSSL_PARAM_construct_uint("bits", &bits);
    params[1] = OSSL_PARAM_construct_uint("primes", &primes);
    params[2] = OSSL_PARAM_construct_end();
    EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_params(pctx, params);

    EVP_PKEY_generate(pctx, &pkey);
    EVP_PKEY_print_private(bio_out, pkey, 0, NULL);
    EVP_PKEY_CTX_free(pctx);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr EVP_RSA_gen 3 ,
.Xr EVP_KEYMGMT 3 ,
.Xr EVP_PKEY 3 ,
